# Finishing the aarch64 CDEF port: what it was worth, and the bug it uncovered

date: 2026-08-07
host: Apple M4 Pro (8 P-core + 4 E-core = 12 logical), macOS 26.5.2, aarch64
rustc: stable, release (lto="fat", codegen-units=1), DEFAULT features
       (bitdepth_8 + bitdepth_16, CHECKED DisjointMut). No -C target-cpu=native.
branch: perf/cdef-neon, off perf/p2-kernels @ 0624dfd.
reference: dav1d 1.5.4 (homebrew), driven with `--framedelay 1` so it runs the
           same threading model we do (tile threading only).
vectors: ~/tmp/rav1d-perf/vec/v4k_8tile.avif (3840x2160 8bpc, 4 tile cols x
         2 tile rows) and v4k_8tile_10b.avif, its 10-bit twin. dav1d decodes
         the matching .ivf from ~/tmp/recon-yard/vec/, verified in the P2
         campaign to be the same key frame repeated.

## nice level

NO `nice` ON ANY TIMED RUN. Darwin maps a positive nice value to background QoS
and distorts wall clock by ~40x. `nice -n 19` is for BUILDS and for the
correctness sweeps only.

## Where the port stood before this

`benchmarks/p2_kernel_profile_2026-08-07.meta` found `cdef_arm.rs` had zero
aarch64 intrinsics; commit 70c1a70 then vectorised the **8bpc filter only**.
That left, on entry here:

  * 8bpc filter          NEON      (70c1a70)
  * 16bpc filter         scalar    all three block shapes, all strength branches
  * direction search     scalar    both bit depths
  * padding              scalar    both bit depths

Freshly profiled at the branch point with `/usr/bin/sample` + the committed
`scripts/perf/sample_selftime.py` / `sample_tree.py`, t=1, inclusive time:

  8bpc  v4k_8tile      cdef 4.86% of a 417 ms/frame decode =  20.3 ms/frame
  10bpc v4k_8tile_10b  cdef 17.32% of a 601 ms/frame decode = 104.0 ms/frame
                       (cdef_filter_block_16bpc_inner alone 15.78% = 94.8 ms)

The 16bpc filter, not the 8bpc one, was the big remaining CDEF cost.

## Correctness first: the port uncovered a live bit-identity bug in 70c1a70

The 8bpc kernel that shipped in 70c1a70 used `8191` as its "no pixel here"
sentinel and folded every tap into `max` unconditionally. The scalar reference
(`src/cdef.rs`) fills with `i16::MIN` and takes `min` with an UNSIGNED compare
and `max` with a SIGNED one, so its sentinel is inert on BOTH sides. With 8191
the sentinel is inert in `min` but sets `max = 8191`, which disables the upper
half of the reference's `iclip`.

That is reachable, not theoretical:

  * `sentinel_must_not_raise_max` (new unit test) searches near-flat content and
    finds a divergence within seconds — first case pri=6 sec=2 dir=6 damping=3,
    HAVE_RIGHT absent: old 82, reference 81.
  * On the FULL dav1d-test-data corpus (783 vectors) under a `__simd_test_log`
    build, which runs the NEON kernel and the scalar reference on every block
    and diffs the pixels, the branch point logs **5 CDEF_MISMATCH across 4
    8-bit vectors**. Few — which is exactly why the three-vector check that
    blessed 70c1a70 saw none.

The same corpus sweep also quantifies the known 16bpc bug (#446, the pri_tap
parity bit read at bit 0 instead of bit `bitdepth_min_8`):

  base @ 0624dfd, 783 vectors, CDEF_MISMATCH by bit depth
    10-bit   139 vectors   401,567 mismatching blocks
    12-bit    46 vectors    43,456
     8-bit     4 vectors          5   <- the sentinel bug above
  perf/cdef-neon HEAD, same sweep
    0 CDEF_MISMATCH, 0 CDEF_DIR_MISMATCH, all 783 vectors

`fix/446-arm-cdef-highbd-pri-tap` (PR #448) is the same one-line fix for the
16bpc half; it is carried here because the 16bpc vector path cannot be made
bit-exact without it. That branch is otherwise untouched.

## Bit identity

* **Per-kernel oracle, full corpus.** `scripts/perf/cdef_oracle_sweep.sh` over
  all 783 dav1d-test-data vectors with `--features __simd_test_log`: 0
  CDEF_MISMATCH and 0 CDEF_DIR_MISMATCH, at every commit of this branch.
  (ITX_MISMATCH 10,228 and MC_PREP_MISMATCH 132,811 are pre-existing drift in
  other kernels and are unchanged from the branch point.)
  The `CDEF_DIR_MISMATCH` oracle is NEW — `cdef_dir_direct` had none, which is
  what the P2 profile meant by the drift inventory being "structurally silent"
  about the direction search.
* **dav1d reference MD5, full corpus.** `cargo test --release --test
  decode_md5_verify -- --test-threads=1`, which compares whole-sequence MD5s
  against dav1d's own published hashes:

      distinct mismatching vectors   base 556  ->  HEAD 464
      regressions (mismatch at HEAD but not at base):  0
      newly matching:                                 92
      test_md5_verify_10bit_quantizer                 FAILED -> ok

  The remaining 464 are the documented pre-existing failures owned by other
  kernels (itx 16bpc, mc); this branch does not touch them.
* **Unit differential tests** (`src/safe_simd/cdef_arm.rs` `mod tests`, run on
  every `cargo test`):
  - `neon_row_matches_oracle_{8,10,12}bpc` — 4,000 randomised blocks each
    against an oracle transcribed from `cdef_filter_block_rust`, sweeping all
    16 edge-flag combinations, all three block shapes, all three strength
    branches, and the whole `(pri_strength, sec_strength, damping)` space
    `src/cdef_apply.rs` can produce, with content biased flat/extreme so the
    min/max clip binds.
  - `constrain_neutralises_sentinel_over_full_param_space` — exhaustive proof
    that a sentinel tap yields `clip == 0` for every strength and damping the
    spec can signal, which is what makes the wrapped signed difference of a
    `0x8000` tap unable to escape into `sum`.
  - `neon_dir_matches_scalar` — 3,009 direction-search cases at 3 bit depths
    including all-flat at five levels, pure horizontal/vertical/diagonal edges
    and three checkerboards, i.e. the cases where every cost is EQUAL and only
    the tie-break decides.
  - `sentinel_must_not_raise_max` — the regression test described above.
* **Frame MD5 across the timed campaign**: every invocation of a given
  (vector, threads) agrees within an arm. 8bpc is unchanged from base
  (`a00c11f4…`); 10bpc changes (`840d4364…` -> `4f218411…`) because the #446
  fix changes 10-bit pixels — the new hash is the one that matches the scalar
  reference and dav1d.

### The tie-break trap, and why it is not a risk here

`cdef_find_dir`'s argmax is `if cost[n] > best_cost` — STRICT — so the LOWEST
index wins a tie, and flat content ties constantly. The port vectorises only
the partial-sum ACCUMULATION (six scatter targets, each a run of consecutive
entries at a row-dependent offset, so one row lands per unaligned
load/add/store). The cost computation and the argmax are left scalar and
byte-identical to `cdef_find_dir_rust`. Eight u32 comparisons were never the
bottleneck; the 512 scalar accumulator updates were.

Partial sums are accumulated in i16 rather than the reference's i32. That is
exact, not an approximation: `px` is in -128..=127 and no partial sum takes more
than 8 terms, so |partial| <= 1024. They are widened to i32 before any squaring.

## Soundness

No `unsafe`, no `#[allow(unsafe_code)]`, `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` intact. No
borrow was widened or removed. The changes go the other way:

* The write borrow is still ONE guard per destination row, exactly `w` pixels
  wide — never a wide guard spanning `(h - 1) * stride + w`.
* The top/bottom padding guards still start at `offset + x_start` and span
  exactly `x_end - x_start`, the exact-window discipline the module documents
  (guarding from `offset` would lock 2 pixels this code never reads, in a row a
  concurrent tile worker may legitimately be writing).
* **Borrow COUNT went down 28 -> 20 per 8x8 block** by folding the block-row
  copy and the two right-context pixels into one guard. Two guards over
  `[0, w)` and `[0, w + 2)` of the SAME row cover exactly the bytes one guard
  over `[0, w + 2)` covers, so this is a strict reduction with zero widening.
* The 12-pixel tap window is a read of `tmp`, a private stack buffer, so it
  never straddles a picture row. The only picture reads are the padding copies,
  and each is bounded to the columns it touches.

## Timed campaign

`scripts/perf/p2_sweep.sh`, arms run back to back inside each cell with the arm
order rotating every round. Raw: `benchmarks/cdef_neon_2026-08-07.tsv`
(round, vector, threads, arm, ms/frame, frame md5, foreign-process count).

The box was NOT idle for the whole campaign — two other agents were building and
testing in sibling worktrees, and macOS `mediaanalysisd` sat at ~184% CPU for
part of it. The strict >25%-CPU discard guard would have livelocked, so the
guard was widened to tolerate those (`TOLERATED=`) and the per-cell foreign
count is recorded in the TSV instead of hidden. The headline statistic is
therefore the **paired per-round ratio**: both arms run inside the same round,
so box state is shared and cancels. Absolute ms/frame carries the contention;
the ratio does not.

A first 3-round pass DID produce a spurious 0.945x at 8bpc t=8. Twelve rounds of
that one cell showed it was round-level box state, not the arm — both arms are
bimodal at ~186 and ~200 ms and the two happened to land on opposite modes.
Median paired ratio over 12 rounds: 1.005. Recorded here because a 3-round
median of a bimodal cell is not enough, and the first pass would have shipped a
fictitious regression.

Final, 5 rounds x 3 reps x 4 iters (median of 15 per cell/arm):

  vector           t     base    HEAD    paired base/HEAD
  v4k_8tile        1    413.5   405.7        1.019
  v4k_8tile        2    261.7   256.7        1.020
  v4k_8tile        4    175.6   172.2        1.020
  v4k_8tile        8    187.9   184.3        1.019
  v4k_8tile_10b    1    601.6   520.9        1.154
  v4k_8tile_10b    2    358.5   314.7        1.140
  v4k_8tile_10b    4    224.9   200.6        1.120
  v4k_8tile_10b    8    214.8   202.3        1.077

8bpc moves only 1.9% because its filter was already NEON at the branch point;
what moved there is the direction search, the padding, and the borrow count.
10bpc moves 7.7-15.4% because its filter was scalar.

Per-step, paired medians:

  step                                              8bpc t=1   10bpc t=1
  16bpc filter + dir search + sentinel fix           1.011       1.165
  + borrow count 28 -> 20, px from tmp, direct stores 1.008       1.013
  + widen_row fixed-size padding copies              1.008       0.995

The last row is an honest mixed result: `widen_row` only touches the 8bpc
padding, so the 16bpc machine code is unchanged, and the consistent -0.5% at
10bpc across all four thread counts is code layout, not work. Kept because the
8bpc side is a real +0.8% and the change is the documented fixed-size-array
pattern.

## Against dav1d

`scripts/perf/p2_dav1d_wall.sh`, same instrument on both sides: each stream is
run at 2 and 20 frames and `total = alpha + beta * frames` fitted so process
startup drops out. dav1d 1.5.4 with `--framedelay 1` (tile threading only, the
model we run). 5 rounds, arms rotated. Raw:
`benchmarks/cdef_neon_dav1d_2026-08-07.tsv` (round, arm, vector, threads, then
the two frame counts and their totals in ms).

  vector           t     base    HEAD   dav1d   base/dav1d   HEAD/dav1d
  v4k_8tile        1    426.8   405.2   259.9        1.64x        1.56x
  v4k_8tile        2    290.7   256.7   133.0        2.29x        2.05x
  v4k_8tile        4    176.2   172.9    66.3        2.67x        2.63x
  v4k_8tile        8    187.2   183.0    37.1        5.06x        4.94x
  v4k_8tile_10b    1    610.3   521.8   267.8        2.30x        1.99x
  v4k_8tile_10b    2    376.2   312.9   136.9        2.82x        2.30x
  v4k_8tile_10b    4    224.4   198.2    67.7        3.34x        2.93x
  v4k_8tile_10b    8    213.4   200.3    39.4        5.52x        5.21x

The ratio columns are per-round paired medians. **This campaign ran with a
sibling agent's timed benchmarks live on the box**, so the absolute ms are
inflated on BOTH sides by roughly 5% — dav1d's 8bpc t=1 measures 259.9 here
against the 246.7 on record in `p2_dav1d_2026-08-07.tsv`, and our own base
measures 426.8 against the interleaved sweep's 413.5. Read the ratios, not the
absolutes, and prefer `cdef_neon_2026-08-07.tsv` for base-vs-head.

10bpc t=1 crossing 2.30x -> 1.99x is the headline: that vector spent 17% of its
decode in a scalar CDEF.

## Where CDEF stands now

`sample` self/inclusive time at t=1 after the port:

  8bpc   cdef 4.86% (20.3 ms/frame)  ->  4.21% (17.3 ms/frame)
  10bpc  cdef 17.32% (104.0 ms)      ->  4.50% (23.6 ms/frame)

The 16bpc filter itself went 94.8 -> ~20 ms/frame. But the 8bpc number barely
moved, and the profile says why: of `cdef_filter_block_8bpc_inner`'s 2.76% of
decode, **the filter arithmetic is 0.22% and DisjointMut traffic is 1.60%**
(`BorrowTracker::add::<false>` 0.62, `DisjointImmutGuard` drop glue 0.43,
`add::<true>` 0.29, `DisjointMutGuard` drop glue 0.26). 10bpc is the same shape
(`slice_as` 0.89 + drop glue 0.39 + `mut_slice_as` 0.35 + drop 0.24 = 1.87%
against 0.24% for the filter row).

**CDEF is now a borrow-count problem, not an arithmetic one** — the same
conclusion `p2_kernel_profile_2026-08-07.meta` reached for the loop filter. The
28 -> 20 fold took the obvious slice; the floor for an 8x8 block is 8 block-row
reads + 2 top + 2 bottom + 8 writes = 20, and getting below it means either a
wide guard (banned under tile threading) or dav1d's `edges == 0xf` path that
reads the 12x12 window straight out of `dst` with no scratch buffer at all.
That second option is the real remaining win and it is a soundness question,
not a SIMD one.

## What did NOT get done

* **The `edges == 0xf` fast path.** dav1d has `cdef_filterX_edged_8bpc_neon`,
  which skips the padding buffer entirely for interior blocks. Not attempted:
  it reads two rows above and below the block directly out of `dst`, which is
  precisely the straddle the module docs warn about, and the read-window shape
  would have to be re-derived against the tile-threading borrow rules first.
* **Vectorising the direction search's cost/argmax.** Deliberate — see the
  tie-break note. `cdef_dir_cost` measures 0.07% of decode.
* **The sentinel fill is unconditional.** `[CDEF_VERY_LARGE; 144]` is written
  for every block including 4x4 chroma ones, which only use a 8x8 window.
  Sizing it by `H` needs the const-generic shape known before padding runs;
  measured cost today is `_platform_memset_pattern16` at 0.04% of decode, so it
  was not worth the restructure.

## Reproducing

  # build
  CARGO_TARGET_DIR=~/tmp/rav1d-cdef/target nice -n 19 \
    cargo build --release --example bench_ab_decode -j 6

  # per-kernel oracle over the whole corpus (~7 min)
  CARGO_TARGET_DIR=~/tmp/rav1d-cdef/target-simdtest nice -n 19 \
    cargo build --release --features __simd_test_log --example decode_md5 -j 6
  scripts/perf/cdef_oracle_sweep.sh \
    ~/tmp/rav1d-cdef/target-simdtest/release/examples/decode_md5 \
    test-vectors/dav1d-test-data ~/tmp/rav1d-cdef/oracle.log
  grep -o '^[A-Z_]*MISMATCH' ~/tmp/rav1d-cdef/oracle.log | sort | uniq -c

  # dav1d reference MD5 (~70 s)
  cargo test --release --test decode_md5_verify -- --test-threads=1

  # timed A/B
  BIN=~/tmp/rav1d-cdef/bin ARMS="base cdefneon3" \
    CELLS="v4k_8tile:1 v4k_8tile:2 v4k_8tile:4 v4k_8tile:8 \
           v4k_8tile_10b:1 v4k_8tile_10b:2 v4k_8tile_10b:4 v4k_8tile_10b:8" \
    scripts/perf/p2_sweep.sh out.tsv 5 3 4

  # profile
  scripts/perf/prof_sample.sh <bin> <vec.avif> 1 sample.txt 200 45
  python3 scripts/perf/sample_tree.py sample.txt cdef
